# 2. ORION Is Model-Agnostic

One of ORION's most important design decisions is that ORION itself is **not the AI model**.

The model is replaceable.

ORION is the system around the model.

The architecture therefore looks approximately like:

```text
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │       USER          │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │     ORION UI        │
                    │ Chat / Dashboard    │
                    │ Tasks / Memory      │
                    │ Tools / Automations │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │    ORION API        │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │ ORCHESTRATION CORE  │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
             ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
             ▼                 ▼                 ▼
       MODEL ROUTER        MEMORY/RAG       TOOL GATEWAY
             │                 │                 │
      ┌──────┴──────┐     ┌────┴─────┐     ┌────┴───────────┐
      │             │     │          │     │                │
   Local AI     Cloud AI  Memory   Knowledge MCP         External APIs
   Ollama       OpenRouter         Base     Tools        Browser/etc.
```

This means ORION could use:

* a tiny local model;
* a larger local model;
* OpenRouter;
* a different provider;
* a future local multimodal model;
* specialist models;
* different models for different tasks.

The user is therefore not locked into one AI provider.

