# 6. What "Ever Learning" Means

One of the most important concepts in ORION is persistent learning.

However, ORION should **not** mean:

> “The AI secretly retrains itself after every conversation.”

That would be dangerous and difficult to control.

Instead, ORION uses several forms of persistent learning.

## Working memory

Information needed for the current task.

Example:

> “We are currently working on the ORION authentication module.”

This exists during the task.

## Episodic memory

Summaries of previous interactions and completed tasks.

Example:

> “The user previously changed the authentication architecture from JWT-only to short-lived access tokens plus refresh tokens.”

## Semantic memory

Durable facts.

Example:

> “The primary ORION database is PostgreSQL.”

## Procedural memory

Reusable workflows.

Example:

> “When preparing releases, run tests → build frontend → run migration checks → create release notes.”

## Knowledge memory

Documents and files indexed into RAG.

Example:

```text
ORION/
Projects/
    project-a/
        architecture.md
        roadmap.md
        requirements.pdf
        source-code/
```

ORION can index that material and retrieve relevant parts when needed.

## Evaluation memory

The system can remember what succeeded or failed in evaluation.

For example:

> “This tool-selection pattern caused repeated failures.”

The improvement system can then test a better approach.

